{"text": "Captain Speaks.\n\nMax ran downstairs. He looked around the living room. There was flour on the sofa. There was a pancake on the ceiling. There was a white cat on the chair.\n\nThe front door opened.\n\n\"Hello, Max!\" said Mrs Bell. \"How was your day? Were the children good?\"\n\n\"Yes!\" said Max. \"They were great. Everything was fine.\"\n\nUpstairs, somebody spoke. It was Captain. The bedroom door was open.\n\n\"This is a disaster!\" said Captain.\n\nMrs Bell looked at Max. Max looked at the floor.\n\n\"Oh no, not again!\" said Captain.\n\nMr Bell looked up. He saw the pancake above his head.\n\n\"Silly cat!\" said Captain.\n\nMrs Bell looked at Biscuit. Biscuit was still a little white.\n\nMax's face was redder than a tomato. He wanted to disappear.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he said. \"I can explain. There was flour. Then there were bubbles. Then the cat went into the garden.\"\n\n\"Never going to get that guitar!\" said Captain.\n\n\"You don't have to pay me,\" said Max quietly.\n\nBut Captain was not finished. He walked up and down in his cage. Then he spoke again. His voice was softer now.\n\n\"You are very brave.\"\n\nMrs Bell stopped. \"Who said that?\"\n\nThen Captain began to sing. He sang very badly. But everyone knew the words.\n\n\"Little boat, little boat, the moon is your light…\"\n\nMr Bell's face changed. He looked at Max.\n\n\"Where did he learn that song?\" he asked.\n\n\"From me,\" said Max. \"I sang it to Finn and Nora. My grandmother taught it to me.\"\n\nTwo small faces appeared at the top of the stairs.\n\n\"Max made pancakes!\" said Finn. \"Max is the best!\" said Nora.\n\nMr Bell went into the living room. He came back with the old guitar.\n\n\"My mother sang that song, too,\" he said. \"I often played it on this guitar. But my hands are too slow now. This guitar needs a new player.\"\n\nHe gave it to Max. Max couldn't speak.\n\nMrs Bell laughed and gave him sixty. \"Are you free next Saturday?\" she asked. \"And every Saturday?\"\n\n\